        "daysAgo": 7,
        "comment": "expanded from UPI, the New York Times, CNN via KESQ, Variety and the California Museum",
        "content": "{{true and funny|source=Clint Eastwood to UPI, 11 October 1986, on Carmel's ice-cream rules: \"Next thing you know, it will be apple pie. Then they will ban Mom.\"}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = Clint Eastwood\n| born = 31 May 1930, San Francisco\n| mayor of = Carmel-by-the-Sea, 1986 to 1988\n| town population then = about 4,500\n| ice-cream permit = the first in 17 months, October 1986\n| Oscars for Unforgiven = best director and best picture, 29 March 1993\n| convention prop = an empty chair, 28 August 2012\n| latest film on the record = Juror No. 2, released 1 November 2024\n}}\n\n'''Clint Eastwood''' is an American actor and filmmaker, born 31 May 1930 in San Francisco, who spent two years as mayor of a town of about 4,500 people and used part of the time on ice cream.<ref name=\"brit\">\"Clint Eastwood\", ''Encyclopaedia Britannica'', facts page, updated 26 September 2026. https://www.britannica.com/facts/Clint-Eastwood</ref><ref name=\"cnn\">\"Clint Eastwood Fast Facts\", CNN, republished by KESQ, 7 May 2025. https://kesq.com/news/national-world/cnn-national/2025/05/07/clint-eastwood-fast-facts-4/</ref><ref name=\"nyt\">Robert Lindsey, \"Carmel-by-the-Sea Journal; Eastwood's Law and (Mostly) Order\", ''The New York Times'', 1 September 1987. https://www.nytimes.com/1987/09/01/us/carmel-by-the-sea-journal-eastwood-s-law-and-mostly-order.html</ref> In 2012 he spoke to an empty chair. The chair was standing in for a president.<ref name=\"cnn\" />\n\n== Ice cream ==\nHe was mayor of Carmel, California, from 1986 to 1988.<ref name=\"cnn\" /> On 11 October 1986, UPI reported that the council had issued an ice-cream parlor permit \"for the first time in 17 months.\" Eastwood's line on the old rule was: \"Next thing you know, it will be apple pie. Then they will ban Mom.\"<ref name=\"upi\">\"Eastwood delivers on sweetest campaign pledge\", UPI, 11 October 1986. https://www.upi.com/Archives/1986/10/11/Eastwood-delivers-on-sweetest-campaign-pledge/4950529387200/</ref>\n\nA year later the ''New York Times'' listed what the mayoralty had changed. The city \"has legalized the sale of ice cream cones, provided more public toilets,\" and moved a stalled library expansion.<ref name=\"nyt\" /> The toilets and the library are in the same sentence as the cones.\n\n== The other job ==\nThe California Museum calls him an accomplished jazz musician and says that if acting had failed \"he would have chosen to be a musician,\" and that he had \"performed in small clubs as a pianist.\" He directed ''Bird'' (1988), about Charlie Parker.<ref name=\"museum\">\"Clint Eastwood\", California Museum. https://californiamuseum.org/inductee/clint-eastwood/</ref> In May 1995 he founded Malpaso Records, a jazz label.<ref name=\"cnn\" />\n\n''Unforgiven'' won him the Academy Awards for best director and best picture on 29 March 1993. The California Museum counts nine nominations and four Oscars for the film.<ref name=\"cnn\" /><ref name=\"museum\" />\n\n== The chair ==\nOn 28 August 2012 he was a surprise guest at the Republican National Convention. CNN's timeline says he \"ad-libs a speech in the form of a conversation by speaking to an empty chair that is 'standing in' for President Barack Obama.\"<ref name=\"cnn\" /> The chair did not answer. That was the bit. Britannica's facts page of 26 September 2026 lists him as 96.<ref name=\"brit\" /> The age is a number on a reference page. The chair is a prop with a date.\n\n== Juror No. 2 ==\nVariety, on 12 September 2024, set ''Juror No. 2'' for an AFI Fest premiere on 27 October 2024 and a limited release on 1 November 2024. AFI's Bob Gazzale told the magazine: \"There is only one Clint Eastwood.\"<ref name=\"var\">\"Clint Eastwood's 'Juror No. 2' Sets Release Date\", ''Variety'', 12 September 2024. https://variety.com/2024/film/news/juror-no-2-release-date-clint-eastwood-1236141905/</ref> Britannica's facts page, updated 26 September 2026, still lists that film among the notable works and does not list a later one.<ref name=\"brit\" /> That is an absence on a list, not a retirement announcement.\n\nThe council issued the ice-cream permit. The convention got the chair.\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:True but improbable]]\n"
