        "daysAgo": 5,
        "comment": "expanded from English Wikipedia, the BBC and Official Charts",
        "content": "{{true and funny|source=Olivia Dean to the BBC, 2 February 2026, on the song Man I Need: I want to make something that when I perform live, it's just gonna be like a Tangfastic.}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = Olivia Dean\n| born, English Wikipedia = 14 March 1999, Haringey, London\n| chart double = 3 October 2025, album and single at number 1\n| album = The Art of Loving\n| Grammy, BBC, 2 February 2026 = best new artist\n| resale prices, BBC = above $1,000, more than 14 times face value\n| Mercury Prize, as of 8 October 2026 = nominated, winner not announced\n}}\n\n'''Olivia Dean''' is a British singer. English Wikipedia, which returned a page, gives the name Olivia Lauryn Dean and the birth line 14 March 1999, Haringey, London.<ref name=\"wiki\">\"Olivia Dean\", English Wikipedia. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olivia_Dean</ref> The BBC, on 2 February 2026, called her 26 and said she was born in Tottenham and raised in Highams Park. Tottenham is inside the borough of Haringey.<ref name=\"bbcgram\">BBC, 2 February 2026, on the Grammy, the song, and the tickets. https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c1ev567ly7qo</ref> The birth date in this article is the Wikipedia line. The rest is the BBC and Official Charts.\n\n== The school and the first stage ==\nThe BBC, on 4 January 2024, reported that at 15 she made a three-hour round trip and enrolled on the theatre course at the BRIT School, having deduced she did not have the correct qualifications to study music, and then switched. Her line was: \"I wanted to go there like hell or high water.\"<ref name=\"bbc24\">BBC, 4 January 2024. https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/entertainment-arts-67714013</ref> The same piece says her first show was in front of 16,000 people at the Sziget Festival in Budapest, with Rudimental, and that she is on their 2019 single \"Adrenaline.\"<ref name=\"bbc24b\">BBC, 4 January 2024, on Sziget and Adrenaline. https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/entertainment-arts-67714013</ref>\n\n== The charts ==\nOfficial Charts says the 2023 debut album ''Messy'' debuted at number 4 and secured a nomination for the Mercury Prize.<ref name=\"charts1\">Olivia Dean, Official Charts, on ''Messy''. https://www.officialcharts.com/artist/61513/olivia-dean/</ref> On 3 October 2025, the same account says, her second studio album, ''The Art of Loving'', debuted straight in at number 1 the same week that her single \"Man I Need\" ascended to the top of the Official Singles Chart. Official Charts called her the first British female solo artist to score that chart double since Adele in 2021.<ref name=\"charts2\">Olivia Dean, Official Charts, on the 3 October 2025 double. https://www.officialcharts.com/artist/61513/olivia-dean/</ref> The chart double date in the Official Charts note is 3 October 2025.\n\n== The Grammy, and the sweet ==\nOn 2 February 2026 the BBC reported that she won the Grammy for best new artist, the first Brit to win best new act since Dua Lipa in 2019. She said: \"I'm up here as a granddaughter of an immigrant.\" She also said: \"I'm a product of bravery.\"<ref name=\"bbcgram\" /> The same piece says \"Man I Need\" was inspired by Michael Jackson's \"The Way You Make Me Feel\" and by \"Haribo's insanely fizzy Tangfastic sweets.\" The sentence she gave the BBC was: \"I want to make something that when I perform live, it's just gonna be like a Tangfastic.\"<ref name=\"bbcgram2\">BBC, 2 February 2026, on the Tangfastic line. https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c1ev567ly7qo</ref> Tangfastic is a Haribo sweet. The comparison is hers.\n\n== The resale ==\nThe BBC reported a 2025-26 ticket dispute in the same 2 February story. She demanded, and obtained, a refund for fans who had bought resale tickets after some were listed at more than 14 times their original face value, with prices rising above $1,000. She called the practice \"disgusting\" and \"vile.\" Ticketmaster then announced a resale cap and refunds of any markup buyers had already paid.<ref name=\"bbcgram3\">BBC, 2 February 2026, on the resale refund. https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c1ev567ly7qo</ref>\n\n== The prize not yet given ==\nThe BBC, on 30 July 2026, said ''The Art of Loving'' won album of the year at both the Brits and the Mobo Awards, and that she is nominated for the Mercury Prize. The winner was to be chosen at Newcastle Utilita Arena on 22 October.<ref name=\"mercury\">BBC, 30 July 2026. https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cq6dmlr1gzeo</ref> As of 8 October 2026 that winner had not been announced. A nomination is not the prize.\n\n== The date, and the age the BBC printed ==\nThe Wikipedia line used for the birth date gives Olivia Lauryn Dean, 14 March 1999, and prints an age of 27 beside Haringey, London, England.<ref name=\"wiki2\">English Wikipedia, the birth line. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olivia_Dean</ref> The BBC, on 2 February 2026, calls her 26 and says she was born in Tottenham and raised in Highams Park.<ref name=\"bbcgram\" /> Tottenham sits inside Haringey, which is the borough on the Wikipedia line. This article takes the day and the year from the Wikipedia page that returned, and takes the February age, the school, the charts context, the Grammy, the sweet, and the tickets from the BBC and from Official Charts. A separate biography page that did not return a body is not used as a second birth record.\n\nThe Mercury choice was still in the future on the research date. The BBC, on 30 July 2026, said the winner would be chosen at Newcastle Utilita Arena on 22 October.<ref name=\"mercury\" /> On 8 October 2026 no winner had been announced, so none is recorded here.\n\nShe wanted the live song to feel like a Tangfastic. The Mercury winner had not been named.\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:True but improbable]]\n"
