        "user": "HansardHannah",
        "daysAgo": 7,
        "comment": "expanded from the BBC and CNN",
        "content": "{{true and funny|source=Karoline Leavitt at her first briefing, as CNN reported it on 29 January 2025: there was about to be $50 million taxpayer dollars that went out the door to fund condoms in Gaza. CNN said the White House offered no evidence.}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = Karoline Leavitt\n| age in the BBC story of 12 August 2026 = 28\n| first briefing, CNN = 28 January 2025, James Brady Press Briefing Room\n| briefing figure = $50 million, condoms in Gaza\n| CNN on that figure = no evidence offered\n| departure, as Trump described it = end of August 2026, then an outside adviser\n| 2022 general election = lost to Chris Pappas\n}}\n\n'''Karoline Leavitt''' was White House press secretary. The BBC, on 12 August 2026, called her 28 and the youngest person to hold the job, and reported that President Trump said she would step down at the end of that month.<ref name=\"bbc\">BBC, 12 August 2026. https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cvglzrvyrz3o</ref> A birth day is not stated on the pages used here. The BBC age on that date is the figure this article uses. Trump's description of the next role, as the BBC carried it, was: \"Karoline will now be one of my top outside advisors, and an influential voice within the Republican Party.\"<ref name=\"bbc2\">BBC, 12 August 2026, on the outside-adviser line. https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cvglzrvyrz3o</ref> The same story said it was not then clear who would replace her.<ref name=\"bbc\" /> No page opened for this article names a successor. No page opened here establishes a later television job.\n\n== Before the briefing room ==\nThe BBC account of 12 August 2026 says that in 2022 she won a Republican primary for Congress in New Hampshire and lost the general election to Democrat Chris Pappas. She then spoke for the fundraising group Maga Inc, and then was national press secretary on Trump's 2024 campaign.<ref name=\"bbc3\">BBC, 12 August 2026, on the 2022 race and the campaign job. https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cvglzrvyrz3o</ref> The same story says that in February 2025 her office took control of the White House press pool, stripping organisational duties from the White House Correspondents' Association, and that the Associated Press was initially barred over the name \"Gulf of America.\"<ref name=\"bbc4\">BBC, 12 August 2026, on the press pool. https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cvglzrvyrz3o</ref>\n\n== $50 million ==\nCNN, published 29 January 2025, reported that her first briefing was in the James Brady Press Briefing Room the previous Tuesday, 28 January 2025. She said a foreign-aid pause had stopped a planned condom purchase. The sentence CNN printed was: \"there was about to be $50 million taxpayer dollars that went out the door to fund condoms in Gaza.\"<ref name=\"cnn\">CNN, 29 January 2025. https://www.cnn.com/2025/01/29/politics/gaza-condoms-fact-check</ref> The first briefing date in the CNN story is 28 January 2025. CNN said the White House offered no evidence for the story. The next day, CNN reported, Trump said the money was \"to buy condoms for Hamas.\"<ref name=\"cnn2\">CNN, 29 January 2025, on the absence of evidence and the next-day line. https://www.cnn.com/2025/01/29/politics/gaza-condoms-fact-check</ref> The figure is in the briefing. The evidence, CNN wrote, was not.\n\n== How long, on the BBC count ==\nThe BBC called her the second-longest-serving press secretary of either Trump term, after Sarah Huckabee Sanders, and said she would be his sixth chief spokesperson across the two terms if the departure held.<ref name=\"bbc5\">BBC, 12 August 2026, on length of service. https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cvglzrvyrz3o</ref> That sentence is conditional on the departure. The story did not name the person who would stand at the podium next. A contributor role at a cable network was not confirmed on any page used here, and this article does not assign one.\n\nThe announcement was 12 August 2026. The end date Trump gave was the end of August. The job he named was outside adviser. Between those two sentences, the BBC left the successor blank.\n\n== What the August story names, and what it does not ==\nThe BBC story of 12 August 2026 is the departure record used here. It gives the age, 28. It gives the end of August. It gives Trump's outside-adviser sentence. It gives the 2022 primary win and the general-election loss to Chris Pappas, the work for Maga Inc, the post as national press secretary on the 2024 campaign, the February 2025 change to the White House press pool, the initial bar on the Associated Press over \"Gulf of America,\" and the comparison with Sarah Huckabee Sanders.<ref name=\"bbc6\">BBC, 12 August 2026, the departure story as a whole. https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cvglzrvyrz3o</ref> It does not print a birth day. It does not print a successor. A later item about a cable-news contributor role did not return a page body and is not used. This article does not invent the next podium or the next employer.\n\nThe briefing is a different document. CNN places it on Tuesday 28 January 2025, in the James Brady Press Briefing Room, and prints the sentence about $50 million taxpayer dollars and condoms in Gaza.<ref name=\"cnn\" /> The governing line in that fact check is: \"The White House offered no evidence for the story.\"<ref name=\"cnn2\" /> The next day, on CNN's account, Trump said the money was \"to buy condoms for Hamas.\"<ref name=\"cnn3\">CNN, 29 January 2025, on the next-day wording. https://www.cnn.com/2025/01/29/politics/gaza-condoms-fact-check</ref> The figure and the absence of evidence are both from that page.\n\nThe figure in the first briefing was $50 million. CNN said the White House offered no evidence.\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:True but improbable]]\n"
