        "user": "HansardHannah",
        "daysAgo": 6,
        "comment": "expanded from Britannica, Billboard, Vulture and Rolling Stone",
        "content": "{{true and funny|source=A Cactus Jack letter, as Vulture reported it on a page updated 9 September 2024: the disputed sales comprised 1,291 actual units with timestamps between 11:45 and 11:59 p.m.}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = Sabrina Carpenter\n| born = 11 May 1999, Quakertown, Pennsylvania\n| full name, Britannica = Sabrina Annlynn Carpenter\n| Short n Sweet debut, Vulture = 362,000 album units\n| disputed units in the Cactus Jack letter = 1,291, between 11:45 and 11:59 p.m.\n| Man's Best Friend, Britannica = August 2025\n}}\n\n'''Sabrina Carpenter''' (born 11 May 1999, Quakertown, Pennsylvania) is an American singer. Britannica, revised 27 September 2026, gives the full name Sabrina Annlynn Carpenter and the birth line \"Sabrina Carpenter (born May 11, 1999, Quakertown, Pennsylvania, U.S.).\"<ref name=\"brit\">Encyclopaedia Britannica, Sabrina Carpenter, revised 27 September 2026. https://www.britannica.com/biography/Sabrina-Carpenter</ref> Billboard, on 17 June 2024, calls her \"Quakertown, Pa.-born.\"<ref name=\"billboard\">Billboard, 17 June 2024. https://www.billboard.com/music/chart-beat/sabrina-carpenter-espresso-please-please-please-global-charts-1235711601/</ref>\n\n== Espresso ==\nBillboard, 17 June 2024, reports that \"Espresso\" reached No. 1 on the Global 200 \"with 109.3 million streams\" in the week of 7 to 13 June, and that the week was \"a fifth week\" at No. 1 on Global Excl. U.S.<ref name=\"billboard\" /> Britannica says \"Please Please Please\" \"topped the charts at number one,\" and that \"Espresso\" \"peaked at number three on the Billboard Hot 100.\"<ref name=\"brit2\">Britannica, on Please Please Please and the Hot 100 peak for Espresso. https://www.britannica.com/biography/Sabrina-Carpenter</ref>\n\nBritannica lists the 2022 album ''emails i can't send'' as \"peaking at number 23 on the Billboard 200.\"<ref name=\"emails\">Britannica, on emails i can't send. https://www.britannica.com/biography/Sabrina-Carpenter</ref>\n\n== Short n' Sweet ==\nBritannica says the album ''Short n' Sweet'' \"debuted at number one on the Billboard 200.\" All 12 songs charted on the Hot 100. \"Taste\" peaked at number two. At the 2025 Grammys the album won best pop vocal album, and \"Espresso\" won best pop solo performance.<ref name=\"sns\">Britannica, on Short n' Sweet and the 2025 Grammys. https://www.britannica.com/biography/Sabrina-Carpenter</ref>\n\nVulture, on a page updated 9 September 2024, says ''Short n' Sweet'' debuted at No. 1 \"with 362,000 album units,\" and that ''Days Before Rodeo'', by Travis Scott, logged \"a little over\" 361,000.<ref name=\"vulture\">Vulture, updated 9 September 2024. https://www.vulture.com/article/travis-scott-sabrina-carpenter-charts.html</ref>\n\n== The 1,291 units ==\nThe same Vulture account says a Cactus Jack letter claimed the disputed sales \"comprised 1,291 actual units verified to have timestamps between 11:45–11:59pm.\" The letter was about missing Travis Scott sales in the week ''Short n' Sweet'' debuted at No. 1.<ref name=\"vulture2\">Vulture, updated 9 September 2024, on the Cactus Jack letter. https://www.vulture.com/article/travis-scott-sabrina-carpenter-charts.html</ref> The 1,291 figure is the claim in that letter, as Vulture reported it. It is not a Luminate correction.<ref name=\"vulture2\" />\n\nRolling Stone, 6 September 2024, reported the Luminate reply: \"We are confident that our numbers are correct in accordance with our processes and methodology.\" Billboard told Rolling Stone it would \"stand behind our chart data and methodology.\" The representatives of Carpenter had not commented in that story.<ref name=\"rs\">Rolling Stone, 6 September 2024. https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-news/travis-scott-questions-integrity-album-chart-sabrina-carpenter-1235097088/</ref> Luminate stood by the published chart.<ref name=\"rs\" />\n\n== August 2025 ==\nBritannica says ''Man's Best Friend'' (August 2025) \"debuted at number one on the Billboard 200,\" and that \"Manchild\" debuted at number one on the Billboard Hot 100.<ref name=\"mbf\">Britannica, on Man's Best Friend and Manchild. https://www.britannica.com/biography/Sabrina-Carpenter</ref> Critics objected to the cover. \"One Scottish domestic violence organization called the cover regressive.\" She said the album was meant to show women \"being in on [their] lack of control.\" The brackets are in the Britannica printing of the sentence.<ref name=\"cover\">Britannica, on the cover dispute. https://www.britannica.com/biography/Sabrina-Carpenter</ref>\n\n== The earlier credit ==\nBritannica says she played Maya Hart on the Disney series ''Girl Meets World'' (2014–17). At 10 she \"placed third in The Next Miley Cyrus Project.\"<ref name=\"early\">Britannica, on Girl Meets World and the 10-year-old placement. https://www.britannica.com/biography/Sabrina-Carpenter</ref> The unit count Vulture prints for the debut week is 362000.\n\nThe letter named 1,291 units between 11:45 and 11:59 p.m. Luminate stood by the chart.\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:True but improbable]]\n"
