        "user": "CubeWatch",
        "daysAgo": 4,
        "comment": "expanded from Britannica, USA Today and Variety",
        "content": "{{true and funny|source=USA Today, 3 February 2008, quoting Paul Thomas Anderson on the milkshake line: I just took this insane concept and used it. The 1924 transcript was not opened.}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = Paul Thomas Anderson\n| born = 26 June 1970, Los Angeles\n| directing Oscar, Variety, 15 March 2026 = first of his career, One Battle After Another\n| film Oscars, Britannica = six, including picture, director, and adapted screenplay\n| milkshake line = his account, USA Today, 3 February 2008\n| first feature, Britannica = Hard Eight, 1996\n}}\n\n'''Paul Thomas Anderson''' (born 26 June 1970, Los Angeles) is an American film director. Britannica, revised 15 September 2026, prints \"Paul Thomas Anderson (born June 26, 1970, Los Angeles, California, U.S.).\"<ref name=\"brit\">Encyclopaedia Britannica, Paul Thomas Anderson, revised 15 September 2026. https://www.britannica.com/biography/Paul-Thomas-Anderson</ref> Britannica says he briefly attended film school at New York University and dropped out. The first feature in that entry is ''Hard Eight'' (1996).<ref name=\"hard\">Britannica, on Hard Eight and the film school. https://www.britannica.com/biography/Paul-Thomas-Anderson</ref>\n\n== There Will Be Blood ==\n''There Will Be Blood'' (2007) is, in the Britannica entry, \"based on the novel Oil! by Upton Sinclair.\" Daniel Day-Lewis won the best-actor Oscar. Anderson was nominated for adapted screenplay and for director. The film was nominated for best picture.<ref name=\"blood\">Britannica, on There Will Be Blood. https://www.britannica.com/biography/Paul-Thomas-Anderson</ref>\n\n== The milkshake, which is his account ==\nUSA Today, 3 February 2008, reported his account of the milkshake lines. He said the lines \"came straight from a transcript he found of the 1924 congressional hearings over the Teapot Dome scandal.\" He attributed the image to Senator Albert Fall. The Fall sentence, as Anderson gave it to USA Today, is \"Sir, if you have a milkshake and I have a milkshake and my straw reaches across the room, I'll end up drinking your milkshake.\" He then said, \"I just took this insane concept and used it.\"<ref name=\"usa\">USA Today, 3 February 2008. http://usatoday30.usatoday.com/life/movies/news/2008-02-03-blood-milkshake_N.htm</ref> The quote is his. A 1924 hearing transcript that contains the word milkshake was not opened. The sentence in print is the sentence he gave the newspaper.<ref name=\"usa\" />\n\n== Phantom Thread and Licorice Pizza ==\n''Phantom Thread'' (2017): Britannica says Day-Lewis plays \"a dressmaker whose pursuit of perfection begets tension.\" Anderson \"earned an Oscar nod for his direction, and the film was nominated for best picture.\"<ref name=\"phantom\">Britannica, on Phantom Thread. https://www.britannica.com/biography/Paul-Thomas-Anderson</ref> ''Licorice Pizza'' (2021): Britannica calls it \"a 1970s coming-of-age dramedy\" with Cooper Hoffman and Alana Haim. It was nominated for best picture, and Anderson was nominated for direction and for original screenplay.<ref name=\"licorice\">Britannica, on Licorice Pizza. https://www.britannica.com/biography/Paul-Thomas-Anderson</ref>\n\n== One Battle After Another ==\nBritannica says \"His most recent film is One Battle After Another (2025).\" It is \"inspired by\" the Thomas Pynchon novel ''Vineland'' (1990) but \"otherwise is a new creation.\" Leonardo DiCaprio plays a former revolutionary. Sean Penn plays Col. Steven J. Lockjaw.<ref name=\"battle\">Britannica, on One Battle After Another. https://www.britannica.com/biography/Paul-Thomas-Anderson</ref>\n\n== 15 March 2026 ==\nVariety, 15 March 2026, reports that he won best director, \"the first of his career,\" and said, \"You make a guy work hard for one of these.\" The film had 13 nominations. He also won adapted screenplay and best picture that night. Variety says, \"Previously, he has been nominated for 11 Oscars … but has never won.\"<ref name=\"variety\">Variety, 15 March 2026. https://variety.com/2026/film/news/paul-thomas-anderson-best-director-oscar-one-battle-after-another-1236686144/</ref> Britannica tally of the same night: the film \"won six Academy Awards, including best picture, best director, and best adapted screenplay.\"<ref name=\"six\">Britannica, on the six Academy Awards. https://www.britannica.com/biography/Paul-Thomas-Anderson</ref> Variety lists the three awards he took that night, the 13 nominations, and the 11 earlier nominations without a win. Britannica lists six awards for the film, including picture, director, and adapted screenplay. Both sentences are printed.\n\nThe earlier nominations in the Britannica entry did not become wins: adapted screenplay and director for There Will Be Blood, with the film up for best picture; direction for Phantom Thread, with that film up for best picture; best picture, direction, and original screenplay for Licorice Pizza. Variety calls the 15 March 2026 directing prize the first of his career and prints the line he said that night. The nomination count Variety prints for the earlier career is 11.\n\nHe said the milkshake line came from a 1924 transcript. The transcript was not opened.\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:True but improbable]]\n"
