        "comment": "expanded from Hatnote, USA Today, The Hollywood Reporter, the pageviews API, the Academy and CBC",
        "content": "{{true and funny|source=The Hollywood Reporter, 16 December 2025: before ''Heated Rivalry'' he was a waiter at The Old Spaghetti Factory in New Westminster, and \"I could stand without falling, and push and waver around the ice, but it wasn't even a true skate.\"}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = Hudson Williams\n| born, Hatnote mirror = 13 February 2001\n| role = Shane Hollander\n| pageviews API, 12 January 2026 = 170,057\n| Hatnote the same day = 166.9K\n| award = Canadian Screen Award, best lead performer, drama, 31 May 2026\n}}\n\n'''Hudson Williams''' is a Canadian actor. The Hatnote mirror of the English Wikipedia lede, on the top-100 page for 12 January 2026, prints: \"Hudson Williams (born February 13, 2001) is a Canadian actor and filmmaker.\"<ref name=\"hat\">Hatnote, English Wikipedia top 100, 12 January 2026. https://top.hatnote.com/en/wikipedia/2026/1/12.html</ref> The birth date in this article is that line. USA Today, on 14 January 2026, calls him 24 and says he was \"raised in Kamloops, British Columbia,\" which fits a birthday in February 2001.<ref name=\"usa\">USA Today, 14 January 2026. https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/2026/01/14/heated-rivalry-show-who-are-connor-storrie-hudson-williams/88184454007/</ref> A separate civil birth record was not opened. He plays Shane Hollander in ''Heated Rivalry''.\n\n== The part and the earlier credits ==\n''The Hollywood Reporter'', on 16 December 2025, describes Shane Hollander as \"an anxious and endlessly endearing Canadian hockey prodigy,\" opposite Connor Storrie's Ilya Rozanov, in a series \"Written and directed for television by\" Jacob Tierney and \"based on Rachel Reid's novel.\"<ref name=\"thr\">''The Hollywood Reporter'', 16 December 2025. https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-features/heated-rivalry-hudson-williams-interview-1236449733/</ref> USA Today lists the earlier screen work as \"small, single-episode roles in CBS' Tracker and CBC Television's Allegiance,\" plus short films he \"also wrote and directed.\" He \"graduated in 2020 from Langara College in Vancouver with a fine art's certificate.\"<ref name=\"usacredits\">USA Today on the earlier credits and Langara College. https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/2026/01/14/heated-rivalry-show-who-are-connor-storrie-hudson-williams/88184454007/</ref> The certificate line is printed with that apostrophe.\n\n== The restaurant and the ice ==\nThe ''Reporter'' interview places the job he had just left: \"Before taking on Heated Rivalry, he was working as a waiter at The Old Spaghetti Factory in New Westminster, British Columbia.\" His line on the pay is \"That was how I paid my rent. It was paycheck to paycheck.\"<ref name=\"thrjob\">''The Hollywood Reporter'' on the restaurant. https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-features/heated-rivalry-hudson-williams-interview-1236449733/</ref> On skating, the same interview quotes him: \"I could stand without falling, and push and waver around the ice, but it wasn't even a true skate.\" The article says he \"did not know how to skate.\"<ref name=\"thrskate\">''The Hollywood Reporter'' on the skating. https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-features/heated-rivalry-hudson-williams-interview-1236449733/</ref> What he expected from the audience, in that interview, was \"I thought I'd maybe get 100,000 [followers] by episode six.\" He also said, \"There's no way to prepare for what this side of things looks like.\"<ref name=\"threxp\">''The Hollywood Reporter'' on the follower estimate. https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-features/heated-rivalry-hudson-williams-interview-1236449733/</ref>\n\n== Two counts of one day ==\nThe Wikimedia REST API, for the English Wikipedia article, all-access and all agents, records \"views\":170057 on 12 January 2026. The surrounding days on that response were 81,364 on 10 January, 85,960 on 11 January, 121,214 on 13 January, and 96,275 on 14 January.<ref name=\"api\">Wikimedia pageviews API, Hudson Williams, daily, 10 to 14 January 2026. https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/metrics/pageviews/per-article/en.wikipedia/all-access/all-agents/Hudson_Williams/daily/20260110/20260114</ref> Hatnote's top-100 page for 12 January lists the article at \"166.9K views.\"<ref name=\"hatviews\">Hatnote top-100 listing for 12 January 2026. https://top.hatnote.com/en/wikipedia/2026/1/12.html</ref> The two public counts differ. The API figure quoted here is 170,057. The API count for 12 January 2026 is 170,057. Hatnote printed 166.9K on the same date. He had thought he might reach 100,000 followers by episode six.\n\n== The award ==\nThe Academy of Canadian Cinema and Television, on 31 May 2026, reported: \"Hudson Williams took home Best Lead Performer, Drama Series.\" The same release says ''Heated Rivalry'' \"sets new Canadian Screen Awards record with 16 wins.\"<ref name=\"acad\">Academy of Canadian Cinema and Television, 31 May 2026. https://www.academy.ca/2026/2026-canadian-screen-award-winners-unveiled/</ref> CBC the same night: \"WINNER: Hudson Williams, Heated Rivalry.\"<ref name=\"cbc\">CBC News, 31 May 2026. https://www.cbc.ca/news/entertainment/2026-canadian-screen-awards-9.7218635</ref> In the December 2025 ''Reporter'' interview he said, of the series, \"It was announced last week that it had been renewed for a season two.\"<ref name=\"thrrenew\">''The Hollywood Reporter'', December 2025 interview, on the renewal. https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-features/heated-rivalry-hudson-williams-interview-1236449733/</ref>\n\nHatnote printed 166.9K. The API printed 170,057. The two counts do not match.\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:True but improbable]]\n"
