        "comment": "expanded from The Hindu, the Indian Express and the Tribune",
        "content": "{{true and funny|source=The Indian Express, on 1984: a 10-year-old boy was paid 500 rupees for a small role in his father's film, a black and white drama called Vetri. The Hindu, on the oath of 10 May 2026: C. Joseph Vijay ennum naan.}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = C. Joseph Vijay\n| office = Chief Minister of Tamil Nadu, sworn in 10 May 2026\n| seats, Indian Express = 108 of 234\n| support shown on 9 May 2026 = 120 MLAs-elect\n| confidence vote, 13 May 2026 = 144\n| 1984 fee, Indian Express = 500 rupees, in Vetri\n| oath fragment, The Hindu = C. Joseph Vijay ennum naan\n}}\n\n'''C. Joseph Vijay''' is Chief Minister of Tamil Nadu. ''The Hindu'' reported that he was sworn in on 10 May 2026.<ref name=\"oath\">\"Joseph Vijay sworn in as Tamil Nadu Chief Minister\", ''The Hindu'', 10 May 2026. https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/tamil-nadu/joseph-vijay-sworn-in-as-tamil-nadu-chief-minister/article70961582.ece</ref> No later story opened here says he has left. ''The Hindu'' used the name C. Joseph Vijay at the oath. ''The Indian Express'' uses Joseph Vijay and the screen name Thalapathy.<ref name=\"ie\">''The Indian Express'', on the party, the seats, and ''Vetri''. https://indianexpress.com/article/entertainment/tamil/from-ilaya-thalapathy-to-chief-minister-the-unusual-journey-of-vijay-10750432/</ref>\n\nA birth day is not in the articles opened here. The Tribune, on 2 February 2024, called him 49.<ref name=\"trib\">''The Tribune'', 2 February 2024. https://www.tribuneindia.com/news/india/thalapathy-vijay-takes-the-political-plunge-eyes-2026-assembly-elections-586686/</ref> ''The Indian Express'' said that in 1984 he was a 10-year-old in the film ''Vetri''.<ref name=\"ie\" /> Those two ages point at 1974. They do not supply a day.\n\n== Two spellings ==\nOn 2 February 2024 ''The Hindu'' reported that he announced a party spelled on that page Tamizhaga Vetri Kazhagam, said it would contest the 2026 Tamil Nadu Assembly election, and said it would not contest or endorse anyone in the 2024 Lok Sabha election.<ref name=\"hindu24\">\"Actor Vijay enters politics, names party Tamizhaga Vetri Kazhagam\", ''The Hindu'', 2 February 2024. https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/tamil-nadu/actor-vijay-enters-politics-names-party-tamizhaga-vetri-kazhagam/article67803830.ece</ref> The Tribune the same day used the same spelling, Tamizhaga Vetri Kazhagam, called him 49, and said an application had been made to the Election Commission.<ref name=\"trib\" /> ''The Indian Express'' dates the political launch to 2 February 2024 and spells the party Tamilaga Vettri Kazhagam.<ref name=\"ie2\">''The Indian Express'', on the February 2024 launch and the fan-club network. https://indianexpress.com/article/entertainment/tamil/from-ilaya-thalapathy-to-chief-minister-the-unusual-journey-of-vijay-10750432/</ref> ''The Hindu'', on the day of the oath in 2026, also spells it Tamilaga Vettri Kazhagam.<ref name=\"oath\" /> The February 2024 copy and the 2026 copy do not spell the name the same way. This article prints both.\n\nThe Express says he turned a fan-club network, \"reportedly tens of thousands of units,\" into the party, and that a welfare body called Vijay Makkal Iyakkam had been formalised in 2009.<ref name=\"ie2\" />\n\n== 500 rupees ==\nThe Express account of the film work begins in 1984: \"a 10-year-old boy was paid 500 rupees for a small role in his father's film, a black and white drama called Vetri.\"<ref name=\"ie3\">''The Indian Express'', on ''Vetri'' and ''Jana Nayagan''. https://indianexpress.com/article/entertainment/tamil/from-ilaya-thalapathy-to-chief-minister-the-unusual-journey-of-vijay-10750432/</ref> The same piece says his last film as lead, ''Jana Nayagan'', was held up by a dispute with the Central Board of Film Certification.<ref name=\"ie3\" /> The fee in the sentence is 500 rupees. The year in the sentence is 1984.\n\n== 108, then 120, then 144 ==\n''The Indian Express'' says polling was on 23 April 2026 and results on 4 May 2026. TVK won 108 of 234 seats, the largest party, in its first election. Vijay won both seats he contested, Perambur in Chennai and Tiruchirappalli East. M. K. Stalin lost Kolathur. Congress left the DMK alliance and backed a TVK-led government.<ref name=\"ie4\">''The Indian Express'', on the seat count. https://indianexpress.com/article/entertainment/tamil/from-ilaya-thalapathy-to-chief-minister-the-unusual-journey-of-vijay-10750432/</ref>\n\n== The oath ==\n''The Hindu'', on 9 May 2026, reported that the governor appointed him chief minister-designate after he showed the support of 120 MLAs-elect in the 234-member House. The paper said that ended almost six decades of alternating DMK and AIADMK rule.<ref name=\"gov\">''The Hindu'', 9 May 2026. https://www.thehindu.com/elections/tamil-nadu-assembly/joseph-vijay-tamil-nadu-governor-meeting-government-formation-may-9-updates/article70959569.ece</ref> The governor named in the oath story is Rajendra Vishwanath Arlekar, at Jawaharlal Nehru Indoor Stadium in Chennai. Vijay wore a black blazer and black trousers. Rahul Gandhi was on the stage. As he commenced uttering \"C. Joseph Vijay ennum naan,\" the crowd erupted.<ref name=\"oath2\">''The Hindu'', 10 May 2026, on the oath line and the clothes. https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/tamil-nadu/joseph-vijay-sworn-in-as-tamil-nadu-chief-minister/article70961582.ece</ref>\n\nOn 13 May 2026 ''The Hindu'' reported a confidence vote of 144, with Congress, CPI, CPI(M), VCK, IUML, 25 AIADMK MLAs, and the AMMK member. He said the party had \"over 34.92% of votes.\"<ref name=\"floor\">''The Hindu'', 13 May 2026. https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/tamil-nadu/tamil-nadu-cm-joseph-vijay-wins-floor-test-in-assembly-comfortably-with-144-votes/article70973260.ece</ref> The confidence vote total printed by The Hindu is 144. In the same speech, as the paper quoted it, he accepted the minority-government label: \"If anyone thinks that this is a minority government, we are happy.\" He said the government would \"safeguard the rights of the minorities.\"<ref name=\"floor2\">''The Hindu'', 13 May 2026, on the minority-government line. https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/tamil-nadu/tamil-nadu-cm-joseph-vijay-wins-floor-test-in-assembly-comfortably-with-144-votes/article70973260.ece</ref>\n\nThe 1984 fee was 500 rupees. The 2026 oath began C. Joseph Vijay ennum naan.\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:True but improbable]]\n"
