        "user": "HansardHannah",
        "daysAgo": 7,
        "comment": "expanded from the FIFA release and the BBC",
        "content": "{{true and funny|source=BBC Sport on the Extraordinary FIFA Congress of 11 December 2024: in all three decisions just one voting option was available, and nations gave their votes by acclamation, clapping in front of their cameras.}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = 2030 FIFA World Cup\n| congress = Wednesday 11 December 2024, virtual\n| member associations convened = 211\n| hosts = Morocco, Portugal and Spain\n| centenary matches = three, one each in Argentina, Paraguay and Uruguay\n| method in the FIFA release = appointment by acclamation\n| BBC account of the vote = one option, clapping at cameras\n| 2034, in the same BBC account = Saudi Arabia, second vote\n}}\n\nThe '''2030 FIFA World Cup''' was assigned on Wednesday 11 December 2024. FIFA's release says Morocco, Portugal and Spain were appointed hosts, and that three centenary celebration matches will take place in Argentina, Paraguay and Uruguay, one in each country.<ref name=\"fifa\">FIFA, \"Extraordinary Congress appoints hosts of 2030 and 2034 editions of FIFA World Cup\", 11 December 2024. https://inside.fifa.com/organisation/congress/media-releases/extraordinary-congress-appoints-hosts-of-2030-and-2034-editions-of-fifa-world-cup</ref> The BBC account of the same congress says the nations voted by acclamation, clapping in front of their cameras, and that just one voting option was available.<ref name=\"bbc\">BBC Sport, on the 11 December 2024 Extraordinary Congress. https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/articles/c1j0p3x0klzo</ref> The pages opened here do not describe groups, venues, or a match calendar. None is stated.\n\nThe congress date on the release is 11 December 2024.\n\n== The release ==\nThe FIFA media release is headed as an Extraordinary Congress. The date on it is Wednesday 11 December 2024. It says all 211 FIFA Member Associations convened virtually. It says Morocco, Portugal and Spain were appointed as the hosts of the FIFA World Cup 2030, with three centenary celebration matches to be held in Argentina, Paraguay and Uruguay, one in each country. It calls the appointment an appointment by acclamation.<ref name=\"fifa\" />\n\nThe release title line is: \"Morocco, Portugal and Spain to host FIFA World Cup 2030.\" A second line is: \"Three centenary celebration matches to take place in Argentina, Paraguay and Uruguay in 2030.\"<ref name=\"fifa2\">FIFA release, 11 December 2024, the host line, the centenary line, the 211 associations, and the acclamation. https://inside.fifa.com/organisation/congress/media-releases/extraordinary-congress-appoints-hosts-of-2030-and-2034-editions-of-fifa-world-cup</ref>\n\nThe hosts in that release are three. The centenary is three matches. The release does not describe a tournament co-hosted by six countries.\n\n== The three hosts ==\nMorocco, Portugal and Spain are the names on the host line. The congress that appointed them was virtual. The number of member associations the release says convened is 211. The word the release uses for how they were appointed is acclamation.<ref name=\"fifa2\" />\n\n== The three matches ==\nThe centenary, in the same release, is not a second host slate. It is three matches in 2030, one in Argentina, one in Paraguay, and one in Uruguay. FIFA's order in that sentence is Argentina, Paraguay and Uruguay.<ref name=\"fifa\" /> The BBC, describing the first of the votes, says the congress \"selected Uruguay, Paraguay and Argentina as the centenary hosts.\"<ref name=\"bbc\" /> The two pages name the same three countries. They do not print the three names in the same order. Both limit the centenary to those three, one match each in the FIFA wording.\n\n== Acclamation ==\nFIFA's noun for the appointment is acclamation.<ref name=\"fifa\" /> The BBC gives the mechanics it observed. \"The hosts for both tournaments, and of the 2030 centenary celebrations, were confirmed via two separate votes. The first selected Uruguay, Paraguay and Argentina as the centenary hosts. The second confirmed the three hosts for 2030, as well as Saudi Arabia being awarded the 2034 tournament.\" \"Nations gave their votes by 'acclamation' — clapping in front of their cameras via their video links. In all three decisions just one voting option was available.\"<ref name=\"bbc2\">BBC Sport, the two votes, the cameras, and the single option. https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/articles/c1j0p3x0klzo</ref>\n\nThe BBC counts two votes for the 2030 decisions it is describing, and it says the same congress also awarded the 2034 tournament to Saudi Arabia in the second of those votes. It then says that in all three decisions only one voting option was available. The gesture it describes is clapping, in front of cameras, on the video links.<ref name=\"bbc3\">BBC Sport, one voting option in all three decisions, and clapping at the cameras. https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/articles/c1j0p3x0klzo</ref>\n\nOne option on the vote. Applause at a camera. That is the procedure the BBC page describes. FIFA's word for the same appointment is acclamation.<ref name=\"fifa3\">FIFA release, the phrase \"appointment by acclamation\". https://inside.fifa.com/organisation/congress/media-releases/extraordinary-congress-appoints-hosts-of-2030-and-2034-editions-of-fifa-world-cup</ref>\n\n== What is not on the pages ==\nThe release names the three hosts and the three centenary countries. The BBC page names the same appointments and describes the clap. Neither page, as opened here, describes groups, venues, or a match calendar beyond that host sentence.<ref name=\"fifa2\" /><ref name=\"bbc2\" /> No group is stated. No stadium is stated. No fixture list is stated.\n\nOne voting option was on the screen. The associations clapped.\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:True but improbable]]\n"
