        "user": "HansardHannah",
        "daysAgo": 6,
        "comment": "expanded from Time, Vogue and the New York Times",
        "content": "{{true and funny|source=Time, on the 21 September 1996 wedding: on 17 July, Mary Jo Ferguson of the Greyfield inn wrote to the National Park Service requesting a permit for a wedding ceremony on Cumberland Island. The chapel cross, Time wrote, consists of two sticks held together by string.}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = Carolyn Bessette Kennedy\n| work = Calvin Klein publicist\n| permit request = 17 July, Mary Jo Ferguson, Greyfield inn\n| permit asked of = National Park Service\n| island = Cumberland Island\n| wedding = 21 September 1996\n| chapel = First African Baptist Church\n| cross = two sticks held together by string\n| dress = pearl-white crepe; Narciso Rodriguez\n| died = 16 July 1999, age 33\n}}\n\n'''Carolyn Bessette Kennedy''' was a Calvin Klein publicist. The wedding Time reported was on 21 September 1996.<ref name=\"time\">''Time'', on the wedding of 21 September 1996. https://time.com/archive/6729720/by-george-he-got-married/</ref> On 17 July, Mary Jo Ferguson of the Greyfield inn wrote to the National Park Service asking for a permit to hold a wedding ceremony on Cumberland Island. Time describes the chapel cross as two sticks held together by string.<ref name=\"time2\">''Time'', the permit request and the chapel. https://time.com/archive/6729720/by-george-he-got-married/</ref> She died in a plane crash on Friday 16 July 1999 as it approached Martha's Vineyard Airport; she was 33.<ref name=\"nyt\">''The New York Times'', 23 July 1999. https://archive.nytimes.com/www.nytimes.com/library/national/regional/072399kennedy-rdp.html</ref>\n\nThe permit request date is 17 July.\n\n== The publicist job ==\nTime, writing about the wedding, says that after a Boston store job she was spotted by some Calvin Klein executives and brought down to the main office in New York City, where she became a publicist.<ref name=\"time\" /> The sequence on that page is a store job in Boston, then the main office in New York, then the publicist job. Narciso Rodriguez later told ''Vogue'' that he met her when they both started to work at Calvin Klein.<ref name=\"vogue\">Narciso Rodriguez to ''Vogue'', 2026. https://www.vogue.com/article/she-was-a-hero-narciso-rodriguez-on-carolyn-bessette-kennedy</ref>\n\n== The permit ==\nThe Park Service paper is the document Time prints. On 17 July, Mary Jo Ferguson of the Greyfield inn wrote a note to the National Park Service requesting a permit to hold a \"wedding ceremony\" on Cumberland Island.<ref name=\"time2\" /> The writer of the note, in Time's account, is Mary Jo Ferguson. The inn is Greyfield. The agency is the National Park Service. The thing requested is a permit. The words Time quotes from the request are \"wedding ceremony\". The place is Cumberland Island. The date of the note is 17 July.<ref name=\"time3\">''Time'', Mary Jo Ferguson, the Greyfield inn, and the National Park Service note. https://time.com/archive/6729720/by-george-he-got-married/</ref>\n\nTime also says they kept the marriage on 21 September a secret from the press, and saved themselves from an attack of helicopters that would have rivaled something out of ''Apocalypse Now''.<ref name=\"time4\">''Time'', the 21 September date and the press blackout. https://time.com/archive/6729720/by-george-he-got-married/</ref> That sentence is about the photographers and the date. The permit is the paper that asked to use the island.\n\n== The chapel ==\nTime calls the place a tiny wooden chapel and identifies it as the First African Baptist Church. \"Its altar is a simple wooden stand, and its cross consists of two sticks held together by string.\"<ref name=\"time5\">''Time'', the First African Baptist Church, the altar, and the cross. https://time.com/archive/6729720/by-george-he-got-married/</ref> The altar, in that sentence, is a wooden stand. The cross, in that sentence, is two sticks. The thing holding the sticks is string.\n\n== The dress ==\nTime describes what she wore: a fluid, bias-cut dress of pearl-white crepe, a veil of silk tulle, and crystal-beaded satin sandals from Manolo Blahnik.<ref name=\"time6\">''Time'', the pearl-white crepe dress, the veil, and the sandals. https://time.com/archive/6729720/by-george-he-got-married/</ref> Narciso Rodriguez, speaking to ''Vogue'' in 2026, described how the dress happened: \"I had given her a couple of ideas, she thought one was too architectural, she pulled the neckline down and a dress was born.\"<ref name=\"vogue\" /> He had been her colleague at Calvin Klein. The dress Time names is pearl-white crepe, bias-cut. The sentence Rodriguez gives for the neckline is his.<ref name=\"vogue2\">''Vogue'', 2026, Rodriguez on the neckline and on meeting her at Calvin Klein. https://www.vogue.com/article/she-was-a-hero-narciso-rodriguez-on-carolyn-bessette-kennedy</ref>\n\n== 21 September 1996 ==\nThe date Time gives for the marriage is 21 September. At the rehearsal dinner, Time writes, Kennedy toasted his bride with the words, \"I am the happiest man alive.\"<ref name=\"time7\">''Time'', the rehearsal dinner toast. https://time.com/archive/6729720/by-george-he-got-married/</ref> The dinner is the rehearsal. The date of the wedding those pages are about is 21 September 1996.\n\nFerguson asked for a permit on 17 July. The cross was two sticks held by string.\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:True but improbable]]\n"
