        "comment": "expanded from the World Bank, the BBC, the Guardian, Geology.com and the numismatic catalog",
        "content": "{{true and funny|source=Numismatic Catalog of Venezuela, on Central Bank notes in bolívares digitales: Decree no. 4.553, gazette No. 42.185 of 6 August 2021, a redenomination by dividing by 1 million from 1 October 2021. The name Bolívar Digital is used to differentiate the unit from the former monetary expression.}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = Venezuela\n| population, 2025, World Bank = 28,516,896\n| record last updated = 13 July 2026\n| indicator = Population, total; country Venezuela, RB\n| interim president, BBC 6 January 2026 = Delcy Rodríguez\n| UN line, 23 September 2026 = elections, no timeline given\n| Angel Falls, Geology.com = 3212 feet, measured 1949 by Perry Lowrey\n| monetary decree = 4.553, divide by 1 million from 1 October 2021\n| catalog name = Bolívar Digital, to differentiate the unit\n}}\n\n'''Venezuela''', in the World Bank series \"Population, total\", had a 2025 value of 28,516,896. The API record for Venezuela, RB, date 2025, was last updated on 13 July 2026.<ref name=\"wb\">World Bank API, indicator SP.POP.TOTL, country VE, most recent value. https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/VE/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&mrnev=1</ref> Press reports opened for January 2026 say Delcy Rodríguez was sworn in as interim president, and they report what Nicolás Maduro told federal judge Alvin Hellerstein. A Guardian account of 23 September 2026 says she told the United Nations there will be elections, and that she gave no timeline.<ref name=\"bbc\">BBC, 6 January 2026. https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/ce8gen8nnvlo</ref><ref name=\"unga\">''The Guardian'' live blog, 23 September 2026, on the UN General Assembly. https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2026/sep/23/united-nations-general-assembly-unga-zelenksyy-trump-xi-china-ukraine-russia-europe-latest-news-updates</ref> Angel Falls is listed by Geology.com at 3212 feet, measured in 1949.<ref name=\"geo\">Geology.com, highest waterfall, citing the 1949 survey. https://geology.com/records/highest-waterfall.shtml</ref> The bolivar entry used here is a redenomination under Decree 4.553.<ref name=\"num\">Numismatic Catalog of Venezuela, Central Bank notes in bolívares digitales. https://www.numismatica.info.ve/en/banknotes/bbcv-bsd.htm</ref>\n\nThe World Bank value field is 28516896.\n\n== The population record ==\nThe address is the World Bank API for country VE and indicator SP.POP.TOTL, most recent value, JSON. The fields returned, as opened, are: indicator \"Population, total\"; country \"Venezuela, RB\"; date \"2025\"; value 28516896; lastupdated 2026-07-13.<ref name=\"wb\" /> The value is 28,516,896. The last-updated date on that record is 13 July 2026.<ref name=\"wb2\">World Bank API record, value 28516896, lastupdated 2026-07-13. https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/VE/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&mrnev=1</ref>\n\nThat is the population figure. No barrels-per-day figure was on a page used here, and none is stated.\n\n== January 2026 ==\nThe BBC, on 6 January 2026, reported: \"Delcy Rodríguez was sworn in as Venezuela's interim president in a parliamentary session that began with demands for the release of ousted leader Nicolás Maduro from US custody.\" The same story said: \"Maduro had insisted he was still president of Venezuela as he pleaded not guilty to four charges of drug trafficking and terrorism.\" The BBC dates the seizure to a US raid the previous Saturday.<ref name=\"bbc\" />\n\n''The Guardian'', 5 January 2026, updated 6 January, reported: \"The deposed Venezuelan president Nicolás Maduro pleaded not guilty to drugs, weapons and 'narco-terrorism' charges on Monday, two days after his capture by US special forces.\" It reported that Maduro, 63, insisted to federal judge Alvin Hellerstein that he was \"still president of my country\", had been illegally \"captured\" at his Caracas home, and was \"a prisoner of war\".<ref name=\"guard\">''The Guardian'', 5 January 2026. https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/jan/05/maduro-pleads-not-guilty</ref> The judge named in that story is Alvin Hellerstein. The age printed there is 63. The phrases in quotation marks are the ones the paper attributes to him in court.<ref name=\"guard2\">''The Guardian'', 5 January 2026, the plea, the judge, and the three phrases. https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/jan/05/maduro-pleads-not-guilty</ref>\n\nThe BBC page and the Guardian page are the accounts used here. Both print the not-guilty plea. Both print his statement that he was still president.\n\n== 23 September 2026 ==\n''The Guardian'' live blog of 23 September 2026, on the UN General Assembly, reported that interim leader Delcy Rodríguez told the UN her country would hold elections. The sentence it quotes is: \"I want to state this very clearly before this general assembly: there will be elections in Venezuela.\" The blog says she gave no timeline. The same blog's summary line is: \"Rodríguez says Venezuela 'is being reborn'.\"<ref name=\"unga\" /> The quotation is the elections sentence. The absence of a date is what the blog reports. No timeline is added here.<ref name=\"unga2\">''The Guardian'', 23 September 2026, the elections sentence and the summary line. https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2026/sep/23/united-nations-general-assembly-unga-zelenksyy-trump-xi-china-ukraine-russia-europe-latest-news-updates</ref>\n\n== The 1949 measurement ==\nGeology.com, citing the 1949 survey, says: \"Angel Falls in Venezuela is the world's tallest waterfall, with a total height of 3212 feet (979 meters). This total height includes a single uninterrupted drop of 2648 feet (807 meters).\" It says the waterfall was measured in 1949 by surveyor Perry Lowrey, during an expedition funded by National Geographic and led by Ruth Robertson.<ref name=\"geo\" /> The total is 3212 feet. The uninterrupted drop is 2648 feet. The surveyor is Perry Lowrey. The year is 1949. The expedition's funder, on that page, is National Geographic. The leader, on that page, is Ruth Robertson.<ref name=\"geo2\">Geology.com, the 3212-foot total, the 2648-foot drop, Perry Lowrey, 1949. https://geology.com/records/highest-waterfall.shtml</ref>\n\n== Decree 4.553 ==\nThe Numismatic Catalog of Venezuela, in the entry for Central Bank notes in bolívares digitales, gives the legal base as \"Decree no. 4.553 published in the official gazette No. 42.185 of August 6th 2021.\" It says that officially the monetary unit is \"Bolívar Soberano\", as a monetary redenomination by dividing the current value by 1 million starting 1 October 2021. It says the name \"Bolívar Digital\" is used officially to differentiate with the former monetary expression.<ref name=\"num\" />\n\nThe decree number is 4.553. The gazette number is 42.185. The gazette date is 6 August 2021. The arithmetic is division by 1 million. The start date is 1 October 2021. The catalog's reason for the name Bolívar Digital is to tell that unit from the former one.<ref name=\"num2\">Numismatic Catalog of Venezuela, Decree 4.553, the division by 1 million, and the name Bolívar Digital. https://www.numismatica.info.ve/en/banknotes/bbcv-bsd.htm</ref>\n\nDecree 4.553 divided the unit by 1 million from 1 October 2021. The catalog says the name Bolívar Digital is there to tell that unit from the old one.\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:True but improbable]]\n"
