        "comment": "expanded from the National Capital Authority and the ABC",
        "content": "{{true and funny|source=National Capital Authority: on 8 October 1908 the House of Representatives selected Yass-Canberra over Dalgety, 39 votes to 33. ABC News, 10 December 2022: three soldiers arrived at Campion on 2 November 1932, withdrew on 8 November, and 2,500 rounds were used.}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = Australia\n| federation = 1 January 1901\n| section 125 = New South Wales, not less than 100 miles from Sydney\n| Dalgety confirmed = Seat of Government Act 1904, 15 August 1904\n| House vote = 8 October 1908, Yass-Canberra 39, Dalgety 33\n| Royal Assent = 14 December 1908, repealing the 1904 Act\n| Campion = 2 November to 8 November 1932\n| rounds = 2,500\n| minister = Defence Minister Sir George Pearce\n}}\n\nThe '''Commonwealth of Australia''' was declared on 1 January 1901. Section 125 of the Constitution, as the National Capital Authority quotes it, says the Seat of Government will \"be in the State of New South Wales, and be distant not less than one hundred miles from Sydney.\"<ref name=\"nca\">National Capital Authority, history of the national capital. https://www.nca.gov.au/history-national-capital</ref> On 8 October 1908 the House of Representatives chose Yass-Canberra over Dalgety, 39 votes to 33.<ref name=\"nca2\">National Capital Authority, the 8 October 1908 division, 39 votes to 33. https://www.nca.gov.au/history-national-capital</ref> In November 1932 the army sent three soldiers to Campion. They withdrew six days later. The ABC account says 2,500 rounds were used.<ref name=\"abc\">ABC News, 10 December 2022, on the 1932 operation at Campion. https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-12-10/great-emu-war-90-years-on-army-wheatbelt-battle-history/101752238</ref>\n\nThe House division on 8 October 1908 was 39 votes to 33.\n\n== 1 January 1901 ==\nThe National Capital Authority timeline begins: \"1 January 1901: Commonwealth of Australia is declared.\" The same entry quotes section 125. The seat of government will be in New South Wales. It will be distant not less than one hundred miles from Sydney.<ref name=\"nca\" /> The distance in the quotation is one hundred miles. The state in the quotation is New South Wales. The city the distance is measured from is Sydney.<ref name=\"nca3\">National Capital Authority, 1 January 1901 and the text of section 125. https://www.nca.gov.au/history-national-capital</ref>\n\n== Dalgety ==\nThe same timeline says: \"15 August 1904: Seat of Government Act, 1904 confirms Dalgety as the site of the future capital.\"<ref name=\"nca4\">National Capital Authority, the Seat of Government Act 1904 and Dalgety. https://www.nca.gov.au/history-national-capital</ref> Dalgety is the site the 1904 Act confirmed. The date of that confirmation, on the authority's page, is 15 August 1904. Four years later the House voted on a different pair of names.\n\n== The division ==\n\"8 October 1908: House of Representatives selects Yass-Canberra over Dalgety as the site for the National Capital, 39 votes to 33.\"<ref name=\"nca2\" /> The chamber is the House of Representatives. The winner on that division is Yass-Canberra. The loser is Dalgety. The count is 39 to 33. The date is 8 October 1908.<ref name=\"nca5\">National Capital Authority, Yass-Canberra, Dalgety, and the 39-to-33 count. https://www.nca.gov.au/history-national-capital</ref>\n\nThe 1904 Act had named Dalgety. The 1908 division named Yass-Canberra. The count on the page is 39 to 33.\n\n== Royal Assent ==\n\"14 December 1908: Seat of Government (Yass-Canberra) 1908 receives Royal Assent. This repeals the 1904 Act.\"<ref name=\"nca6\">National Capital Authority, Royal Assent on 14 December 1908 and the repeal of the 1904 Act. https://www.nca.gov.au/history-national-capital</ref> The assent is 14 December 1908. The title of the new Act, on that line, is Seat of Government (Yass-Canberra) 1908. The authority says the new Act repeals the 1904 Act. Dalgety had been the site in the statute. After 14 December 1908 the statute the authority describes is the Yass-Canberra Act.\n\n== Campion ==\nABC News, on 10 December 2022, drawing on 1932 newspaper reports and on pictures supplied by the National Library of Australia and the Australian War Memorial, gives the operation as follows. \"Three soldiers from the Royal Australian Artillery arrived in Campion on November 2, 1932.\" \"When the army withdrew on November 8 after six days of battle, 2,500 rounds of ammunition had been used to kill between 300 and 500 emus.\" The piece says the minister who sent them was Defence Minister Sir George Pearce.<ref name=\"abc\" />\n\nThe arrival date is 2 November 1932. The withdrawal date is 8 November 1932. The unit, in the ABC sentence, is the Royal Australian Artillery. The number of soldiers in that sentence is three. The ammunition figure is 2,500 rounds. The range the ABC gives for emus killed is 300 to 500. The minister named is Sir George Pearce.<ref name=\"abc2\">ABC News, 10 December 2022, the dates, the rounds, the range, and the minister. https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-12-10/great-emu-war-90-years-on-army-wheatbelt-battle-history/101752238</ref>\n\nThe House voted 39 to 33. The artillery took 2,500 rounds to Campion and left on 8 November.\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:True but improbable]]\n"
