        "daysAgo": 7,
        "comment": "expanded from the Justice Department consolidation, Statistics Canada and the food inspection agency",
        "content": "{{true and funny|source=Department of Justice consolidation of the Constitution Act, 1867, endnote (4): \"The first day of July, 1867, was fixed by proclamation dated May 22, 1867.\" Canadian Food Inspection Agency: mandatory information on consumer prepackaged food must be shown in French and English.}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = Canada\n| Act dated = 29 March 1867\n| section 3 = One Dominion under the Name of Canada\n| proclamation = 22 May 1867, fixing 1 July 1867\n| section 91 class 24 = Indians, and Lands reserved for the Indians\n| Constitution Act 1982 = section 35\n| population, 1 July 2026 = 41,798,407\n| Daily release = 23 September 2026\n| prepackaged food = mandatory information in English and French\n}}\n\nThe '''Constitution Act, 1867''' is dated \"(29th March 1867).\" Section 3 says \"the Provinces of Canada, Nova Scotia, and New Brunswick shall form and be One Dominion under the Name of Canada.\" Endnote (4) in the Department of Justice consolidation says: \"The first day of July, 1867, was fixed by proclamation dated May 22, 1867.\"<ref name=\"const\">Department of Justice, Constitution Acts, 1867 to 1982, full text. https://www.laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/const/FullText.html</ref> Statistics Canada, in ''The Daily'' of 23 September 2026, estimated the population at 41,798,407 on 1 July 2026.<ref name=\"stat\">Statistics Canada, ''The Daily'', 23 September 2026. https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/260923/dq260923a-eng.htm</ref> The Canadian Food Inspection Agency requires mandatory information on consumer prepackaged food in both official languages.<ref name=\"cfia\">Canadian Food Inspection Agency, bilingual food labelling. https://inspection.canada.ca/en/food-labels/labelling/industry/bilingual-food-labelling</ref>\n\nThe Daily figure for 1 July 2026 is 41798407.\n\n== The Act ==\nThe consolidation prints the date of the Act as 29 March 1867. Section 3 names three provinces: Canada, Nova Scotia, and New Brunswick. The verb in the section is \"shall form and be\". The style in the section is \"One Dominion under the Name of Canada.\"<ref name=\"const\" /> The name of the country, in that section, is Canada. The date on the Act is not the date in the endnote.<ref name=\"const2\">Department of Justice, Constitution Act, 1867, the dating clause and section 3. https://www.laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/const/FullText.html</ref>\n\n== The proclamation ==\nEndnote (4) is one sentence. \"The first day of July, 1867, was fixed by proclamation dated May 22, 1867.\"<ref name=\"const3\">Department of Justice, Constitution Act, 1867, endnote (4). https://www.laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/const/FullText.html</ref> The day the endnote fixes is 1 July 1867. The instrument that fixes it is a proclamation. The date of that proclamation is 22 May 1867. The Act itself is dated 29 March 1867. The proclamation sits between the date on the Act and the day the endnote names.\n\n== Section 91 and section 35 ==\nSection 91, class 24, of the 1867 Act gives the federal Parliament exclusive authority over \"Indians, and Lands reserved for the Indians.\"<ref name=\"const4\">Department of Justice, Constitution Act, 1867, section 91, class 24. https://www.laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/const/FullText.html</ref> The class number is 24. The authority it assigns is exclusive, and it assigns that authority to the federal Parliament.\n\nThe Constitution Act, 1982, section 35(1), in the same consolidation, says: \"The existing aboriginal and treaty rights of the aboriginal peoples of Canada are hereby recognized and affirmed.\" Section 35(2) says: \"aboriginal peoples of Canada includes the Indian, Inuit and Métis peoples of Canada.\"<ref name=\"const5\">Department of Justice, Constitution Act, 1982, section 35(1) and section 35(2). https://www.laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/const/FullText.html</ref> Subsection (1) recognizes and affirms existing aboriginal and treaty rights. Subsection (2) defines aboriginal peoples of Canada as including the Indian, Inuit and Métis peoples of Canada.\n\n== The Daily ==\n''The Daily'', released 23 September 2026, says: \"The population of Canada reached an estimated 41,798,407 people on July 1, 2026, an increase of 189,425 people (+0.5%) from July 1, 2025.\" It says this is the lowest annual July-to-July growth in the total size of the population since 1944/1945, which it gives as +126,000 and +1.0 percent, and the lowest growth in percentage since 1915/1916, which it gives as +20,000 and +0.3 percent. The parenthetical in the release notes that the comparison is June to June before 1946.<ref name=\"stat\" /> The count is 41,798,407. The date of the count is 1 July 2026. The release date is 23 September 2026. The increase from 1 July 2025 is 189,425 people, 0.5 percent.<ref name=\"stat2\">Statistics Canada, ''The Daily'', 23 September 2026, the count, the increase, and the two comparison years. https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/260923/dq260923a-eng.htm</ref>\n\n== The label ==\nThe Canadian Food Inspection Agency page on bilingual labelling says: \"Mandatory information on consumer prepackaged food must be shown in both official languages, that is, French and English.\" It says this includes core labelling requirements, such as the common name, and prescribed words or expressions for specific foods. The citations it prints are section 206 of the Safe Food for Canadians Regulations and section B.01.012(2) of the Food and Drug Regulations.<ref name=\"cfia\" /> The mandatory words on a prepackaged food, including the common name, have to appear in English and in French. The local-market exceptions are the ones set out in those regulations.<ref name=\"cfia2\">Canadian Food Inspection Agency, the common-name rule and the two regulation citations. https://inspection.canada.ca/en/food-labels/labelling/industry/bilingual-food-labelling</ref>\n\nThe Act is dated 29 March 1867. A proclamation dated 22 May 1867 fixed 1 July. A prepackaged food still has to carry the common name in English and in French.\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:True but improbable]]\n"
