        "comment": "expanded from the Television Academy, Time, Variety and CNN",
        "content": "{{true and funny|source=Time, 12 August 2014: \"Mork was weird — popping-out-of-an-egg, rainbow-suspenders, scat-riffing-about-the-Shah-of-Iran weird.\" Ron Clements, in Variety, 28 January 2021: six to eight four-hour sessions, and everything was transcribed.}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = Robin Williams\n| Juilliard = 20 students in the class\n| left the school = 1975\n| Comic Relief = 29 March 1986, three hours\n| Oscar = Good Will Hunting, 1997\n| Aladdin sessions, Clements = six to eight\n}}\n\n'''Robin Williams''' was one of 20 students in his freshman class at the Juilliard School. The Television Academy says he and Christopher Reeve \"were the only two chosen for the advanced program overseen by John Houseman.\" On ''Happy Days'' he was \"cast in a guest role as Mork,\" and \"it spawned a popular spin-off, Mork and Mindy.\" For ''Good Will Hunting'' \"he won the award for best supporting actor in 1997.\" ''Mrs. Doubtfire'' is in the Academy's list of the films.<ref name=\"tva\">Television Academy, biography of Robin Williams. https://www.televisionacademy.com/bios/robin-williams</ref> On 7 November 2014 the Marin County coroner ruled the death a suicide, CNN reported.<ref name=\"cnn\">CNN, 7 November 2014, on the Marin County coroner. https://www.cnn.com/2014/11/07/showbiz/robin-williams-autopsy</ref>\n\n== The egg ==\n''Time'', James Poniewozik, on 12 August 2014, described the character in one line: \"Mork was weird — popping-out-of-an-egg, rainbow-suspenders, scat-riffing-about-the-Shah-of-Iran weird.\" In a 2011 interview printed with that piece, the exit from school is dated. Asked \"you left Juilliard in, what? 1975?\" he answers, \"Five, yeah,\" and \"I left school, I fell in love, and went back to live in San Francisco.\"<ref name=\"time\">''Time'', 12 August 2014. https://time.com/3102128/robin-williams-dead-mork-remembrance/</ref> The entrance was an egg. The suspenders were rainbow. The year he gives for leaving is 1975.\n\n== Three hours ==\nThe Academy notes that with \"Billy Crystal and Whoopi Goldberg, he hosted several Comic Relief specials to raise funds and awareness to help people in need, especially the homeless.\"<ref name=\"tvacr\">Television Academy on Comic Relief. https://www.televisionacademy.com/bios/robin-williams</ref> Variety, quoting the producers, dates the first one: the three hosts were booked \"for the inaugural three-hour 'Comic Relief' telecast on March 29, 1986.\" Bob Zmuda: \"Always keep a camera on Robin, because he's the one who's going to be bouncing around all the time and you never know what he's going to do.\" HBO co-founder Chris Albrecht: \"Comic Relief 'couldn't have happened without the three of them.'\"<ref name=\"varcr\">Variety on the 29 March 1986 telecast. https://variety.com/2014/tv/news/comic-relief-campaign-was-more-than-photo-op-for-robin-williams-1201282307/</ref> The booking was three hours. The date was 29 March 1986. The instruction Zmuda gives is to keep a camera on him.\n\n== The sessions ==\nThere is no Disney archive page in hand that states a single retained-hour total for the ''Aladdin'' recordings. What co-director Ron Clements told Variety on 28 January 2021 is a count of sessions. \"On the first day of recording\" one scene, \"by the 25th (and last) take, the scene had expanded to about 20 minutes long.\" \"By the end of that first four-hour session, Robin was dripping with sweat.\" \"Robin ended up recording about six to eight four-hour sessions.\" \"Everything Robin recorded was transcribed.\" \"there was a tremendous amount of strong material that didn't make it.\"<ref name=\"varal\">Ron Clements, in Variety, 28 January 2021. https://variety.com/2021/film/spotlight/aladdin-robin-williams-1234893550/</ref> The session count Clements gives is six to eight.\n\n== The list ==\nCNN's career graph says \"He won an Oscar for playing a therapist in the 1997 film 'Good Will Hunting,'\" and lists \"Mrs. Doubtfire\" among the \"huge box-office hits.\"<ref name=\"cnnoscar\">CNN, 7 November 2014, on the Oscar and on ''Mrs. Doubtfire''. https://www.cnn.com/2014/11/07/showbiz/robin-williams-autopsy</ref> The Television Academy places the same prize as best supporting actor in 1997 and places ''Mrs. Doubtfire'' in its list of the films.<ref name=\"tvaoscar\">Television Academy on the 1997 award and the film list. https://www.televisionacademy.com/bios/robin-williams</ref> The ''Aladdin'' count is not on those two pages. It is Clements in Variety on 28 January 2021: about six to eight four-hour sessions, a first scene that reached about 20 minutes on the 25th take, sweat by the end of the first session, and a transcript of everything recorded. A single retained-hour total from a Disney archive was not on a page opened here.\n\nEverything he recorded was transcribed. The first scene ran to about 20 minutes by take 25. No single hour total was on a page opened here.\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:True but improbable]]\n"
